            <ArticleTitle>Animal toxicity of hairpin pyrrole-imidazole polyamides varies with the turn unit.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>7449-57</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1021/jm401100s</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A hairpin pyrrole-imidazole polyamide (1) targeted to the androgen receptor consensus half-site was found to exert antitumor effects against prostate cancer xenografts. A previous animal study showed that 1, which has a chiral amine at the a-position of the ?-aminobutyric acid turn (?-turn), did not exhibit toxicity at doses less than 10 mg/kg. In the same study, a polyamide with an acetamide at the ß-position of the ?-turn resulted in animal morbidity at 2.3 mg/kg. To identify structural motifs that cause animal toxicity, we synthesized polyamides 1-4 with variations at the a- and ß-positions in the ?-turn. Weight loss, histopathology, and serum chemistry were analyzed in mice post-treatment. While serum concentration was similar for all four polyamides after injection, dose-limiting liver toxicity was only observed for three polyamides. Polyamide 3, with an a-acetamide, caused no significant evidence of rodent toxicity and retains activity against LNCaP xenografts.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
